d "5ec409c9-9da8-57e6-8151-dac8668f101e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089783 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186089784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089783 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186089785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089783 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186089786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089783 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186089787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089783 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186089788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089783 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089783 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089783 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e01a288e-35f8-510c-acf4-1aa5f2ab35bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089784 #edb/ref 1080 "None" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089784 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089784 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "95c08e8a-1307-50cd-ba4f-1a01312f41c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089785 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089785 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089785 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bdf66758-ab71-5d6c-a9e5-7c18a9fc9c30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089786 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089786 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089786 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "32d94768-4265-593e-832b-d26f7c44faea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089787 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089787 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089787 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4f70ae2d-1356-5d67-85e2-39e80c4783b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089788 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089788 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186089788 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "753fe3fc-8deb-5cef-a28b-89461d1928e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091944 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12766" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091944 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/45/45a423930fb07c365f10cdfbdeb3d45ef337078fa77e1b0699388088438d4c70.png)\n\nWhat is the subset $A$ of $U=\\{1,2,3,4,5 \\}$ whose indicator function $\\boldsymbol{1}_A(x)$ is shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091944 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2acf7f4e-1d5d-56e5-8478-9611a3afde73" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091944 #edb/ref 1074 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1$, depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X$. Then, the indicator function of a set $A \\subseteq X$ is the function\n${1,\\begin{vmatrix}if [math]x \\in A[/math], \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}if [math]x \\notin A[/math]$.\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $1,2$, and $5$ are mapped to $1$. Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set\n\n$$\nA=\\{1,2,5 \\}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091945 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12767" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091945 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\mathbf{1}_{A}(-1) = 0, \\qquad \\mathbf{1}_{B}(-1) = 1$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{1}_{A \\,\\cup\\, B}\\,(-1) = 1$\n2. $\\mathbf{1}_{A \\,\\cup\\, B}\\,(-1) = 0$\n3. $-1 \\in A \\cup B$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091945 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cc45cc10-61e3-5cb7-babc-7253a5158c4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186091945 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overline{A}$, $A \\cap B$, and $A \\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1_{A^(―)}(x) &= 1 - 1_{A}(x) \\\\\n1_{A∩B}(x) &= min{1_{A}(x), 1_{B}(x)} \\\\\n1_{A∪B}(x) &= max{1_{A}(x), 1_{B}(x)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1_{A∪B}(-1) &= max{1_{A}(-1), 1_{B}(-1)} \\\\\n&= max{0, 1} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\mathbf{1}_{A \\,\\cup\\, B}\\,(-1) = 1$, we conclude that $-1$ belongs to $A \\cup B$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092338 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092338 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092338 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092338 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the Indicator Function of a Set" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092338 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186091944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092338 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2e380013-0002-5a17-bbf7-e9ce89e6331d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092339 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092339 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092339 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092339 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092339 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092339 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186092604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092339 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186376157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092339 #edb/ref 1039 "Using Properties of Indicator Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092339 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186091945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092339 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "bd399539-33cf-5738-b970-fcc6c1d919c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092441 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092441 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092441 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092441 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092441 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092441 #edb/ref 1045 "Indicator Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092441 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186093249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092441 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092441 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "32f8f5ef-15f3-5904-a759-74a6bc8770e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092600 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186089771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092600 #edb/ref 1069 "q-172706" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092600 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b2/b2919328788c6762d1a0b56225b95749e88973ec84803584be0d336f63033566.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the subset $A$ of $U = {a, b, c, d, e}$ whose indicator function $1_{A}(x)$ is shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092600 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c3c4858f-5a41-55b3-bb89-5356156d99e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092602 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186089783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092602 #edb/ref 1069 "q-172721" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092602 #edb/ref 1071 "$1_{A}(5) = 1, 1_{B}(5) = 0$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $1_{A∩B}(5) = 1$\n2. $1_{A∩B}(5) = 0$\n3. $5 ∈ A∩B$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186092602 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dbfb19de-ef84-5bc1-a24a-a4632bbb8c2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093249 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093249 #edb/ref 1061 46177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093249 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a653fede-ccbc-5f2f-b061-934db5fa0d21" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093249 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093250 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093250 #edb/ref 1061 46178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093250 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7390f8dd-a510-5070-8b26-59db8798d82a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093250 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093251 #edb/ref 1061 46181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093251 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d6d114da-0075-5e74-9dfb-15ec23ed1bd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093251 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186092339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093252 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093252 #edb/ref 1061 46185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093252 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d765dcbb-6e69-5616-b71c-25e2c514aa3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093252 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186093496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093495 #edb/ref 1064 "An **indicator function** is a function that maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1$, depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset.\n\nThe indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X$. Then, the indicator function of a set $A \\subseteq X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1,\\begin{vmatrix}if [math]x \\in A[/math], \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}if [math]x \\notin A[/math]\n$$\n\nFor example, let\n\n$$\nA = \\{a,c \\}\n$$\n\nbe a subset of the universal set\n\n$$\nX=\\{a,b,c,d,e \\}\n$$\n\nThen, the indicator function $\\mathbf{1}_A(x)$ of $A$ is the following:\n\n![](images/7b/7b1ec0a80d5ec7dc9974d2ebaf6984e94faa00d73ec624dc48d56addbe701ee1.png)\n\nNotice that the elements $a$ and $c$ (the elements of our subset) are mapped to $1$, and all other elements are mapped to $0$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093495 #edb/ref 1065 8006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093495 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "20986866-6719-520f-b707-cf6b26e3823b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093495 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093496 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that the indicator function of a set $A \\subseteq X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1,\\begin{vmatrix}if [math]x \\in A[/math], \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}if [math]x \\notin A[/math]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the indicator function of the complement $\\overline{A}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{1,\\begin{vmatrix}if [math]x \\in \\overline{A}[/math], \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}if [math]x \\notin \\overline{A}[/math]\n$$\n\nThis means that the indicator function of $A$ and its complement are related as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1_{A^(―)}(x) &= 1 - 1_{A}(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the indicator functions of $A \\cap B$ and union $A \\cup B$ can be expressed through the indicator functions of $A$ and $B$.\n\n- For the indicator function of the *intersection*, we have:\n$1_{A∩B}(x)|= min{1_{A}(x), 1_{B}(x)}; = 1_{A}(x) \\cdot 1_{B}(x)$\n- For the indicator function of the *union*, we have\n$1_{A∪B}(x)|= max{1_{A}(x), 1_{B}(x)}; = 1_{A}(x) + 1_{B}(x) - 1_{A}(x) \\cdot 1_{B}(x)$.\n\nWe can prove these results by splitting them into cases." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093496 #edb/ref 1065 8007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093496 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a111c445-39e3-5ddd-8272-48ef3795a3b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186093496 #edb/ref 1067 "Properties of Indicator Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
